WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.203
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy-nominated comedian

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:04.737
who is hosting
"Saturday Night Live" this week,

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:07.206
right here on NBC,
with musical guest Foo Fighters.

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:10.777
Please welcome back
to the show Nate Bargatze.

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:13.313
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:29.295
Welcome back, man.
-What's up, man?

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:31.130
-I'm so --
-Thank you.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.799
-Hey, bear!
-Hey, bear. Hello, folks.

00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:35.034
-I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.302
I'm so happy the reason
you're in New York --

00:00:37.302 --> 00:00:38.371
you're hosting "SNL."

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.973
-Yes.
-This is such a blast.

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:42.041
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:44.143
It's -- Yeah, it's insane, dude.

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:47.113
It was like -- It's almost --
When they asked, you're like,

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.782
"I don't know if I'm famous
enough to do that."

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:52.052
Like, you want to -- Almost,
I want to tell them that.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.119
They're like,
"We're going to get you."

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.920
I was like,
"Guys, I appreciate it,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.790
but there's some other people
I think y'all should go after."

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.091
You're like,
"Your show has a legacy.

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:00.159
You don't want to mess
around with that."

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:01.593
-You're "SNL, dude."

00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:04.931
I go, "Look, thanks for
the offer, but, you know...

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.032
I'm doing you a favor."

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:07.233
-You've played -- Obviously,

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.501
you've played
New York City a ton.

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:11.270
You've played big rooms
in New York City a ton.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.073
How are the nerves
on Tuesday here?

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:15.741
-So far, it's good.

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:17.343
This was the first day --
I mean, you know what?

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:22.448
They called me Sunday
about, like, a sketch idea.

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:24.417
And I was like, "Oh, man,
it's, like, happening."

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:25.852
Like, it was Sunday.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:28.821
-And then, Monday, you come in,
and, I mean, you really --

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:30.456
I'm really waiting
for them to be like,

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:32.058
"We're not going to air this."

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:34.927
Like, they're -- Like,
I'm just some trial show

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:36.195
that they're like,

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:39.065
"We just wanted to make sure
the cameras worked."

00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:41.200
Someone -- Like,
Vin Diesel's backstage.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.501
And I'm like, "Is he on?"

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:43.669
And you're like,
"What's he doing?"

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:45.171
You're like,
"Don't worry about him."

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:47.106
-He just doesn't like
doing dress anymore.

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:48.274
And you're like,
"These are a lot of

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.342
Vin Diesel-type sketches."

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:52.745
-I don't know if I
can sell these, dude.

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:56.115
-I will say, when I was there
on the writing staff,

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:59.118
the greatest feeling was
when a stand-up would show up

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.821
because you knew they would
handle their own monologue.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.322
Do you --
Is that safe to assume

00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:04.723
that you were going to
write your own monologue?

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:07.893
-Yeah, yeah. That's the only
thing I can do that's good.

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:11.530
Like, I ran it last night.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.565
It was great
because I got to, like --

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:13.733
You know,
I've been on the road,

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:14.934
so it's like you're doing
an hour, and then --

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.837
So, last night, I came in,
and last night

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.905
I ran around and did spots

00:02:19.905 --> 00:02:21.207
and, you know, trying to
figure out --

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.674
The one idea I thought I was
going to do --

00:02:22.674 --> 00:02:24.076
it's kind of changed.

00:02:24.076 --> 00:02:25.311
And so then you're like --
You're just kind of trying to

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.680
figure out the --
you know, rough eight minutes

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.315
or so that we're going to work.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:30.883
-When you
run around New York City,

00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:32.485
so you're going to different
clubs, you're doing spots.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:34.420
Do you let them know, "Hey,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.889
I'm going to be doing this
on Saturday at 'SNL.'"

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:38.724
Do you give them that preface?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:43.229
-The host said -- I mean, like,
if I go up and go,

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.997
"This will be on 'SNL.'"

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:46.499
And then they go...

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.100
I would, just go,
"Just so you know,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:52.672
this is on 'Saturday Night Live'
this Saturday."

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:55.841
It's -- You do --
I mean, I remember

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:57.310
following a lot of comics
that would come in

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.613
and run in for a spot,
and so it's very cool.

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.681
You do want the audience
to know -- I would do it

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:04.584
with late-night sets,
when you'd run those to be like,

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:05.951
"Just tell them
this is what I'm doing."

00:03:05.951 --> 00:03:07.787
So the audience understands
you're doing 5 minutes

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:08.788
or 8 minutes, whatever it is.

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:09.856
It's very tight.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:11.724
It's just a little
different feeling.

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:13.493
But you have to get back
into the New York rhythm.

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:15.794
Like, the New York rhythm --
Because when I lived here,

00:03:15.794 --> 00:03:17.329
it's very --
You know, it's, like, quick.

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:19.265
It's like you've got to be
just on top of them.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.667
And, you know, instead of, like,
when you do a long show,

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.002
it's, like, a little more.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:26.772
And so, I mean, I had to,
like -- I did five sets,

00:03:26.772 --> 00:03:31.677
and it took me a good three
just to get back into New York.

00:03:31.677 --> 00:03:33.946
One of them did not go good.
-Yeah.

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:36.816
-It's so funny because people
will say to me sometimes,

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:38.617
"Oh, my God,
I can't even imagine

00:03:38.617 --> 00:03:40.386
what it's like to go onstage
and do an hour."

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:43.523
And you're like, "Weirdly,
it's easier than doing five."

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:44.591
-Yes.

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:46.292
-Because an hour,
they get to know you.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.827
You know,
realize they're not seeing

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.661
the best five minutes first.

00:03:49.661 --> 00:03:51.631
But when you've got to do five,
they're like, "Wait.

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.099
This is the five?"

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.867
-Yeah, this is it.

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:56.802
They go, "You're doing
that this Saturday?"

00:03:56.802 --> 00:04:00.206
I get a couple of that.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:03.242
They're going, "You're doing
this on -- Alright."

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:04.677
-And they're like,
"Honey, I think we can

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:06.045
wake up early on Sunday,

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:09.115
because we're going to get
a good night's sleep."

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.783
-People will just tune in
for the sketches.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:11.884
They're like,
"We'll watch the sketch."

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.020
It's like,
that's the worst I'm at.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.423
They're like, "But we've seen
this other, so..."

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:20.226
-Now, a lot of people might
think you're flying high

00:04:20.226 --> 00:04:22.929
because you're hosting "SNL,"
but you had an allergic reaction

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:24.230
in Wichita.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.264
Was this before a show?

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:27.066
-This was an hour
before the show.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:28.400
-Okay.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.236
-I tried to go running,
tried to do the right thing

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.971
for my dumb body.

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.839
That's what's infuriating,
because you go,

00:04:33.839 --> 00:04:36.075
"I'm doing what
I'm supposed to do, man,"

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.344
and then your body falls --
Like, I don't --

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:40.746
I don't remember ever
being allergic to anything.

00:04:40.746 --> 00:04:43.916
And I guess you just get
old and weak

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:47.220
and you can't handle air and...

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.954
-So, this is --
You posted a photo of yourself.

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:51.357
You look pretty, uh, yeah.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.692
Was it mid-run that you realized
it was happening or...

00:04:53.692 --> 00:04:55.461
-We did --
We were having to do two laps.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.631
So, it was mid-run, we go back.
-Those are those mile laps?

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:01.434
-Yeah. Yeah, mile.

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:05.471
We do two laps around the bus.
And...

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:08.374
-It's a big bus.
-It's a big bus, everybody.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:10.209
So, I go back.

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:12.411
My hands start itching.
My feet are itching.

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:15.381
And it's Wichita, which has
some of the most allergies.

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:17.649
Like, you know, and those people
are some strong people

00:05:17.649 --> 00:05:19.852
because they're breathing in
that stuff.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:22.054
And it was, like, ragweed.
I guess it just hit me wrong.

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:24.390
So that goes up -- I mean,
we're an hour before the show,

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:25.491
and it's like this.

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:26.959
I was like, "This is not good."

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:29.161
And, so, then my buddy is like,
"You should take Benadryl."

00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:30.863
But I was like, "That's going
to make me fall asleep.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:32.064
You know,
I don't want to take it."

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.699
We got an EMT came down,

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:35.601
and he was a super-nice guy,
bless his heart.

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.369
But it was his first day.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.369 --> 00:05:39.639
-And so he was not --

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:41.507
He doesn't have
the stuff for this.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.442
-Yeah.
-And, so, he just came in --

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:45.043
-By the way,
that's a different person.

00:05:45.043 --> 00:05:46.345
-It's a different person.

00:05:46.345 --> 00:05:47.980
And, so, he came in
and was like, "I don't know."

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.114
You know...

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:51.483
And then he didn't know --

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:53.653
Like, you know,
if you work at --

00:05:53.653 --> 00:05:54.754
you don't know who everybody is.

00:05:54.754 --> 00:05:55.955
-Sure.

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:58.056
-And, so, he comes down there
and then he goes,

00:05:58.056 --> 00:05:59.792
"So, your name is
Travis Mathew,"

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.928 line:0%
because I have this shirt on.

00:06:03.662 --> 00:06:06.031 line:0%
It's just the brand.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:07.800
And I just said, "Yeah."

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.802
I just went with it
because it's --

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:11.503
You know, am I going to go
into a whole thing about it?

00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:13.940
You know, I was like, "Just
write, yeah, Travis Mathew."

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.107
You know?

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:18.210
Like, "I'm Ralph Lauren. I am.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.979
My friends call me Polo."

00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:24.484
So, I'll just do
whatever that guy said.

00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:27.186
-That's weirdly -- The most
insulting thing is he thought

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:29.354
you were a guy who would just
have your own name.

00:06:29.354 --> 00:06:32.724
-Yeah. That into my shirt to --

00:06:32.724 --> 00:06:35.494
We ended up getting --
Another doctor came, Dr. Sean.

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:39.031
Goes-by-first-name-only
doctor, so that's --

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:40.700
-Those are the guys you get
at venues right before a show.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.968
-Yeah.

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:43.069
-They're, like,
first-name doctors.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:44.136
-Those, you got to be like,

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:46.405
"Where's the bottle
for these pills?"

00:06:46.405 --> 00:06:49.241
And there's just no bottle,
because it's all --

00:06:49.241 --> 00:06:52.844
He goes, "I got a few things
down here."

00:06:52.844 --> 00:06:55.181
-Yeah, Dr. Sean doesn't
like follow-up questions.

00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:58.617
-Yeah, he doesn't.
He goes, "Dr. Sean."

00:06:58.617 --> 00:07:03.455
I go, "Can I see your ID?
He goes, "I left it in the car."

00:07:03.455 --> 00:07:05.124
But he was very nice, too.

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:06.125
And, like, he gave --
And I ended up

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:07.259
having to take Benadryl.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:08.427
Then I fall asleep.

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:10.996
And, so, then I had to
give my assistant --

00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:12.197
I was like, "You got
to wake me up, dude,

00:07:12.197 --> 00:07:13.566
like, if I fall asleep."

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:15.834
And I fell asleep hard
because of Benadryl.

00:07:15.834 --> 00:07:17.303
And then I did -- And I got up.

00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:19.404
I mean, I was --
Within eight minutes

00:07:19.404 --> 00:07:22.208
of being the most asleep,

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:25.277
I was just in front
of 6,000 people.

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:28.547
-And were you cognizant
of that whole act of like,

00:07:28.547 --> 00:07:30.048
"I'm out of it," or did you --

00:07:30.048 --> 00:07:31.250
once you
got in front of the audience,

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:33.052
did it kind of lock in,
like muscle memory?

00:07:33.052 --> 00:07:34.920
-Yeah, it went in.
You know what?

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.188
The show was great.

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:38.757
I almost think it was
a lot more freeing.

00:07:38.757 --> 00:07:40.559
Like, I was all --
You know, I'm on Benadryl.

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:42.127
I'm like -- I'm up there like,

00:07:42.127 --> 00:07:44.229
"We're having a pretty good time
tonight, folks."

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:47.900
Like, I go, "Nice and easy,"
you know?

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.335
My wife told me --

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:51.771
Because I was telling her this,
and she said --

00:07:51.771 --> 00:07:52.971
Because we're worried
about being nervous

00:07:52.971 --> 00:07:54.106
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:55.240
And she was like,
"Well, if you can do this,

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:56.575
you might be all right."

00:07:56.575 --> 00:08:00.312
Like, just to wake up and
then go talk to 6,000 people.

00:08:00.312 --> 00:08:01.647
But they were awesome.

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:04.283
-I will tell you, after --
If between dress and air,

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.351
you'll know dress
didn't go well if Lorne goes,

00:08:06.351 --> 00:08:07.753
"Maybe some Benadryl?

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:09.621
Do you want it?"
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:09.621 --> 00:08:10.823
A whole bottle.

00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:12.591
-He goes,
"I've heard Benadryl helps."

00:08:12.591 --> 00:08:14.860
-Dr. Sean's there.
I go, "How'd you find him?"

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.061
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:18.631
Oh, trust me, Lorne's got
a bunch of Dr. Seans.

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:23.969
You mentioned your wife.

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:27.639
I've always talked about
I'm so such a fan

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:31.310
of the material you do
about her onstage.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.879
She does not seem to be someone
who puts you on a pedestal,

00:08:33.879 --> 00:08:35.280
based on the material
you do about it.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.784
Is she excited, though,
about Saturday?

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:40.953
-Very excited.
This one's very exciting.

00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:43.022
I mean, we've been together
before I ever started comedy,

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:47.126
so it's -- You know, I think
that's a big reason for that.

00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:50.095
And -- But she's very excited
about "Saturday Night Live."

00:08:50.095 --> 00:08:54.132
This is definitely something.
It really is crazy.

00:08:54.132 --> 00:08:56.402
It's -- I mean,
I can't even des--

00:08:56.402 --> 00:08:59.738
Like, you're just really shocked
that I'm even here.

00:08:59.738 --> 00:09:02.875
And so everybody has been --
Everybody is super-excited.

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:05.278
She's a big fan
of the Foo Fighters, as I am --

00:09:05.278 --> 00:09:06.746
-Yes. That's an exciting thing,
as well.

00:09:06.746 --> 00:09:09.548
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:09.548 --> 00:09:12.084
That was probably her most
excitement, was that.

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:13.585
Like, I feel like
that's what impressed her.

00:09:13.585 --> 00:09:15.187
-Yeah.
-I didn't even choose.

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:18.457
It's like I went in and go,
"I'd like the Foo Fighters."

00:09:18.457 --> 00:09:21.694
This is my first time hosting.
And the Foo Fighters, please.

00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:23.028
-She heard that.
She was like,

00:09:23.028 --> 00:09:24.596
"Oh, that's how they can get
away with you hosting."

00:09:24.596 --> 00:09:26.065
-Yeah, exactly.
-"They got the Foo Fighters."

00:09:26.065 --> 00:09:28.801
-Oh, yeah. It's going to be
all Dave Grohl in every sketch.

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:32.771
I'm just kind of on the show.

00:09:32.771 --> 00:09:35.874
Yeah, so, very excited.
I get a lot of sketch ideas.

00:09:35.874 --> 00:09:37.943
Do you get --
-Oh, from, like, family and --

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:40.378
-Oh, just every -- I mean,
this is everybody's dream

00:09:40.378 --> 00:09:42.648
is to somehow have an in.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:44.216
Because, I mean,
I know it because I've --

00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:45.618
You've watched "Saturday Night
Live" your whole life,

00:09:45.618 --> 00:09:47.652
and you're like, "I've got
a pretty good sketch idea."

00:09:47.652 --> 00:09:49.488
And so then once
you get a friend

00:09:49.488 --> 00:09:51.023
that's like, "You know,
I'm going to be there,"

00:09:51.023 --> 00:09:54.026
they're like,
"Well, let me let it fly."

00:09:54.026 --> 00:09:57.429
And it's just been text
and e-mail of just like,

00:09:57.429 --> 00:09:58.630
"Run this by everybody."

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:02.234
Like, it just --
Nobody's a professional writer.

00:10:02.234 --> 00:10:05.604
-Anything helpful or just does
it all feel like spam mail?

00:10:05.604 --> 00:10:07.673
-It's all --
Like, I'm going through it.

00:10:07.673 --> 00:10:09.641
I mean, some are like,
"Yeah, that's funny."

00:10:09.641 --> 00:10:11.611
Then some are like, "Dude,
who do you think we are?

00:10:11.611 --> 00:10:13.312
Like, you think I can just
walk in to

00:10:13.312 --> 00:10:14.981
'Saturday Night Live,'
professional writers,

00:10:14.981 --> 00:10:19.384
and just be like,
'Hey, my buddy Michael --

00:10:19.384 --> 00:10:22.721
he took some improv
classes 40 years ago

00:10:22.721 --> 00:10:24.790
and he's got some good ideas.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.092
I'd like to --
I got him on the horn

00:10:27.092 --> 00:10:29.895
if y'all want to talk to him.'"

00:10:29.895 --> 00:10:32.431
-Well, I hope that they do talk
to Michael just for that, yeah.

00:10:32.431 --> 00:10:34.633
-I, do too. Yeah.
-Hey, I'm so excited.

00:10:34.633 --> 00:10:36.401
I think everybody's
going to be so excited.

00:10:36.401 --> 00:10:38.670
Thanks so much for making time
for us on the busy week.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.005
-You're the best, man.

00:10:40.005 --> 00:10:43.275
-You guys, Nate Bargatze,
"SNL" Saturday, October 28th,

00:10:43.275 --> 00:10:45.477
with the Foo Fighters,
on NBC and live on Peacock.

00:10:45.477 --> 00:10:46.645
Stick around.

00:10:46.645 --> 00:10:47.947
We'll be right back
with Andrew Rannells.

